I have a shell script I call "bmake" it has the following line:


( nohup make $* & ) 2>&1 >make.$LOGNAME 


This functions like 'make' only your make happens in the background and
your output goes into ./make.$LOGNAME assuming LOGNAME is set to your
login id.  This could be changed to make.out but then if you have more than
one person compiling in the same directory you loose your output.

To watch your output as it happens type:

tail -f make.___

Example:

where the login id is 'fred'
and 'bmake' is in your PATH.

$ cd src
$ bmake
$ tail -f make.fred

You can interrupt this tail and even logout and the make will continue until
it either dies of errors or finishes.

Now for your problem:  You could start over and check it in the morning
                       using this program.  Then you will have all the output
                       sitting in a file ready to have its errors posted here
                       for help.


Hope this helps solve some of the problems.
